# Never force push — always apply

An agent does not force push, in this repository or any repository it routes to that does not
prescribe its own branch-update convention, in any of the forms it takes: `--force`,
`--force-with-lease`, a `+refs/heads/...` refspec, or a plain `git push` of a branch whose history a
rebase, an amend, or a squash has already rewritten. Move a published branch forward with a merge
of the new base or a correcting commit. If the rewrite has already happened locally, do not push
it: name the branch, name what the rewrite would break, and leave the decision with the human.

Rewriting a published branch discards work no one in the session can see. This has been measured:
an automated restack force-pushed two stacked branches once their base merged, and the dependent
pull request came back conflicting with roughly ten add/add conflicts across files that should
have merged cleanly — the history relationship was gone, not the content. The engineer who found
it: "it should never force push - that can override people's work, make it hard to review".

## Where the sub-repository governs instead

[`sub-repo-agents-md`](sub-repo-agents-md.mdc) already settles the crossing: repo-local standards
win for work done in that repo, and this repository's job is routing rather than authoring another
repo's rules. A repository whose own `AGENTS.md` prescribes how its published branches are updated
is outside this rule. Follow that repository's instruction; nothing in the Forbidden list below
binds there.

Read the repository's `AGENTS.md` before concluding it is silent — that check is
`sub-repo-agents-md`'s first step, not an extra one this rule adds. A rebase-then-`--force-with-lease`
workflow is a convention a repository may legitimately hold; where one does, follow it.

## Forbidden

Where this rule binds — this repository, and any repository that prescribes nothing:

- Any force push, `--force-with-lease` included. The lease guards against a remote update you have
  not fetched; it does nothing about a dependent branch or a stacked pull request, which is the
  damage this rule exists to prevent.
- The same act under another spelling: deleting the remote branch and pushing it again, pushing a
  freshly built branch over the old name, or a `+refs/heads/...` refspec.
- Rebasing a branch that already exists on a remote. Merge the new base into it instead, so the
  ancestry its dependent branches and its review are built on survives.
- Reporting a rewrite as done when what remains is a force push the agent will not make. Name the
  branch, name what the rewrite would break, and leave the decision with the human.

Everywhere, including a repository that governs itself:

- Rewriting a branch that is not solely yours — one with a stacked pull request on it, a dependent
  branch, or another author's commits. That is the case the incident above measured, and no
  repo-local convention licenses it.
